wer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170551" :question/id #uuid "7ac2dbe7-d30d-532d-9aae-ed5a7b744bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-170551" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12x+8y+3=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12x+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12(0)+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}+4y]+3 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y+2)^{2}-4]+3 & =0 \\\\ 2(y+2)^{2}-8+3 & =0 \\\\ 2(y+2)^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}})$ and $(0,-2+\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170551/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170551"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-9471"] :question/problem "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}(0)-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[{y}^{2}-2\\sqrt{5}y]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-5]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-15+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/d37e546bf2b17c99c87650e7c0973a3a881c8c5f3a8ed28f5f923d9d3d500511.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5214"] :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5213"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/4527514f6b522bf5922bcba4b77a1574b29f65c238c8b3dd976d6af6bb6c01ae.png)\n\nCalculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x -  6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x-6y & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-4x-6\\cdot 0 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x+4)-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5220"] :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ y-3 & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/c090d0410cca8d00f0f93641a4f8bfbfa66c3b586894797e5c5c89291d11126d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5221"] :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20x-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot {0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20\\cdot 0-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2({y}^{2}-4y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-4y+4-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y-2)^{2}-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{(y-2)}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-2)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/dc56aec7e7c16c4fcfee77f7126f53351c1513c4bfb5e64ac41834ed30ee5782.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
